    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-69990-0",
    "abstract": "The Hadley circulation plays a key role in transporting atmospheric heat from the tropics to the subtropics, shaping the climate in low latitudes. While its projected weakening has been largely attributed to rising greenhouse gas concentrations, the long-term impact of future anthropogenic aerosol concentrations remains underexplored. Here, we show that the projected changes in anthropogenic aerosol concentrations can account for about one-third of the Northern Hemisphere Hadley circulation weakening between 1980 and 2080, emerging as a major driver of tropical circulation change throughout the 21<sup>st</sup> century. This impact is linked to altered diabatic heating patterns in the northern tropics, driven by precipitation changes under reduced aerosol forcing. These results highlight a critical trade-off of aerosol mitigation: while improving air quality, it may amplify tropical atmospheric circulation changes driven by greenhouse gases.",
    "field": "Atmospheric circulation dynamics",
    "problem": "While the weakening of the Hadley circulation is largely attributed to greenhouse gases, the long-term impact of changing anthropogenic aerosol concentrations on this weakening is not well quantified, and their relative importance and underlying mechanisms remain poorly understood.",
    "method": "This study uses large ensembles of single-forcing climate model simulations and the Kuo-Eliassen equation to isolate the impact of declining anthropogenic aerosols on the weakening of the Northern Hemisphere Hadley circulation and to diagnose the underlying physical mechanisms.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the contribution of declining anthropogenic aerosols to the weakening of the Northern Hemisphere Hadley circulation in the 21st century and to identify the underlying physical mechanisms.",
